{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01YaJYu7dBPPFzAceQ2Rtm8Q","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The new `pub(crate)` and private methods are unused since they're not yet called. But wait, `start_probe_bw_refill` and `is_reno_coexistence_probe_time` are called by `check_time_to_probe_bw`. And `check_time_to_probe_bw`, `check_time_to_cruise`, `check_startup_resume` are `pub(crate)` which means they could be called from within the crate. But since they're not called anywhere yet, Rust/clippy considers them \"never used\".\n\n\nThe dead_code warnings for methods like `has_elapsed_in_phase` and `enter_startup_resume` were already present, but they only become errors when running clippy with `-D warnings` to treat all warnings as errors.\n\nLooking at the actual clippy output, only my new methods are flagged as errors—the existing methods with the same issue aren't being promoted to errors in the same way. I could use `#[allow(dead_code)]` on the new functions, though that feels like a workaround rather than addressing the root cause of why these methods exist but aren't being called. So the issue is that `dead_code` lint flags unused functions regardless of visibility level—even `pub(crate)` methods get caught if they're never actually called anywhere in the code. The visibility doesn't protect them from being marked as dead code; it just affects whether they're part of the public API surface. Looking at the warning output, I can see both the existing `pub(crate)` methods and my new private methods are flagged as unused. This suggests the existing codebase might already be failing clippy with `-D warnings`, or there's something about how the test configuration affects which warnings get reported. Let me check if the original code passes clippy without my changes to understand what's actually expected here.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"in